====================================================================
DECISION / NEXT
====================================================================

ADR 0028 is a success on its stated scope: reduce noise to a
tractable minimum without touching enumeration correctness. The
output is now something an upper-layer ("name the theory," "pick
canonical rules," "compose into a concept") could reasonably consume.

The follow-ups that would tighten this further — all outside ADR 0028:

  1. **Compositional subsumption.** Detect that, under the presence
     of one axiom X, another axiom Y is entailed. On the equivalence
     case this would collapse the four transitivity variants to one.
     Requires SAT/SMT-style reasoning; nontrivial.
  2. **Conjunctive naming.** Name a pair/triple of axioms as a
     single named concept (e.g., "equivalence" = {sym, trans, refl}).
     This is the "theory-object" step the user identified in
     feedback: going from rule-detection to concept construction.
  3. **Meta-R encoding of axioms.** Currently axioms are Rust values,
     not R instances. Persisting them in R would complete the
     commitment-3 picture ("types are meta-R") for rules as well as
     patterns.

Of these, (2) is the natural successor — it's the step from
"individual axioms" to "theory-level structure." (1) would help (2)
by making its input cleaner on edge cases. (3) is a representational
question; can come later.
